## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses end-to-end testing competencies for distributed feature flags, including concurrency handling, cache invalidation strategies, rollout semantics, and system recovery mechanisms.

## Key Concepts
- Concurrency and contention under high read/write load
- Cache invalidation strategies (TTL-based, write-through, event-driven)
- Gradual rollout strategies (percentage-based, regional, user-segment)
- Test data generation and observability for flag state transitions
- Chaos engineering and rollback safety mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simulate flag resolution path under concurrent load
async function resolveFlag(userId, flagKey) {
  const cached = await redis.get(`flag:${flagKey}`);
  if (cached !== null) {
    metrics.increment('flag_cache_hit');
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  }
  const flag = await mongo.flags.findOne({ key: flagKey });
  await redis.setex(`flag:${flagKey}`, 300, JSON.stringify(flag));
  metrics.increment('flag_cache_miss');
  return flag;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model slow network partitions and database failures?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect flag propagation lag?
- How would you validate flag consistency across a multi-region deployment?

